term ZIndexStackingContext {
  id: "@community/term-z-index-stacking-context"
  version: "1.0.0"

  meaning: "A stacking context is a three-dimensional conceptualization of HTML elements along an imaginary z-axis perpendicular to the viewport. The `z-index` property is meaningful only WITHIN a stacking context — child elements with a higher z-index than another sibling will paint on top, but a stacking context boundary isolates that ordering from the rest of the page. Misunderstanding stacking contexts is the most common cause of 'why is z-index: 9999 still behind something' bugs."

  domain: frontend-engineering

  _meta: {
    license: "Apache-2.0"
    license-provenance: "package-authorship-claim"
  }


  aliases: ["stacking context", "z-index context", "compositing layer (informally)"]

  notes: [
    "A new stacking context is created by ANY of: `position` other than `static` with `z-index` not `auto`; `opacity < 1`; `transform`, `filter`, `will-change` (with non-`auto`); `isolation: isolate`; `mix-blend-mode` other than `normal`; flex/grid items with `z-index`; `contain: layout/paint/strict`; `position: fixed/sticky`.",
    "The CSS spec is W3C CSS Positioned Layout Module Level 3, building on CSS 2.1 §9.9 (Layered presentation).",
    "Inside a stacking context, paint order is: (1) root background, (2) negative-z descendants, (3) block-level non-positioned descendants, (4) floats, (5) inline non-positioned descendants, (6) z-index: auto / 0 positioned descendants, (7) positive-z positioned descendants — in that order.",
    "Common bug: `transform: translateZ(0)` on a parent unintentionally creates a stacking context that traps the modal's z-index. Use `isolation: isolate` deliberately for explicit context creation.",
    "Modal/dropdown libraries use React portals (or equivalent) to render OUTSIDE any ancestor stacking context — typically directly under `<body>` — so z-index works as expected.",
    "Best practice: define a global z-index scale in tokens (see @community/value-z-index-tokens), reserve high z-index values for portaled overlays only, and avoid arbitrary `z-index: 9999`.",
    "Devtools: in Chrome, the 'Layers' panel shows compositing layers and can help diagnose unexpected stacking contexts.",
  ]

  related: [
    @community/value-z-index-tokens,
    @community/collection-accessible-modal,
    @community/anti-pattern-magic-numbers,
    @community/anti-pattern-hardcoded-pixel-values,
  ]
}
